  "title": "BLOOMCORE",
  "summary": "I’ve always been a little bit afraid of plants. I love them, of course, but on some ancient instinct, I have a dreadful respect for their vegetable will. This might be because of <em><a href=\"https://www.are.na/block/2359767\">The Man Whom The Trees Loved</a></em>, a horror story I read at an impressionable age, by the British gothic writer Algernon Blackwood. In the story, a young man senses a murky intelligence deep beneath the bark of a cedar. This special living “something” in the tree excites him; it feels almost animal-like. He can’t shake it, and the obsession becomes overwhelming, ecstatic. He fantasizes that the forest might someday “engulf human vitality into the immense whirlpool of its own vast dreaming life.” The monstrous forest at the edge of his ordered cottage garden creeps closer and closer, until it claims him whole. \n\nBlackwood’s story stirs something deep in me. When the tendrils of ivy creeping along my back fence touch the ground, I rush to cut them back, lest they take me away too. On a whim I recently Googled “dendrophobia,” the fear of trees. In the years-spanning comments section of an online phobia dictionary — a place I consider to be the Deep Internet, almost primevally online — I read about a man stricken to paralysis by the gnarled roots of banyan trees “haphazardly touching the ground in one strangling embrace.” The whooshing sound of leaves in the wind is a common trigger. Another account, from a young mother: her preschooler noticed a tree near his playground for the first time, and, convinced it wasn’t there yesterday, is now inconsolable. \t\t\n\t\t\t\t\nThe toddler isn’t wrong; plants are always watching us. They’re alive, and even though they can appear static, they’re always on the move, especially, it seems, when we’re not paying attention. As the botanist Jack Schultz writes, plants are “<a href=\"https://mospace.umsystem.edu/xmlui/handle/10355/6759\">just very slow animals</a>.” Forests migrate across great distances, racing agains...",
